## 🧠 Part 1: Multi-Head Causal Self-Attention Framework (Primary Focus)

### 1. Parallel Panel of Experts (Horizontal, Not Sequential!)
- **Identical Input $X$**: The input tensor $X = X_{\text{Embedding}} + PE$ is passed into Head 1, Head 2, Head 3, and Head 4 **simultaneously at the exact same moment**.
- **No Head Feeds Another**: Multi-Head Attention is **100% parallel (horizontal)**. Head 1 does NOT feed into Head 2!
- **Analogy**: 4 GPO supply chain specialists examining the exact same audit log $X$ simultaneously in a conference room. Each specialist writes an independent report ($head\_out_i$), which is concatenated and passed to the Chief Auditor ($W_O$) to make a final decision.

### 2. Parameter Accounting & Matrix Projection Shapes
- **Single-Head Projection**: $W_Q, W_K, W_V \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times 16}$. 16 rows = input dimensions ($D_{\text{in}}$), 16 cols = output Query features ($D_{\text{out}}$). Total $= 256$ params per matrix.
- **4-Head Projection**: $W_Q^i, W_K^i, W_V^i \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times 4}$. 16 rows = input dimensions ($D_{\text{in}}$), 4 cols = head dimension ($d_{\text{head}} = 16/4 = 4$). Total across 4 heads $= 4 \times (16 \times 4) = 256$ params!
- **Strict Parameter Equality**: Single-Head and 4-Head models both have **strictly 1,024 attention parameters** ($d_{\text{model}} = 16$). Any multi-head gain is purely due to multi-subspace representation!


### 3. Single-Head vs Multi-Head Attention Core Comparison
- **Single-Head Model**: Allocates all 16 dimensions to a single head. Squeezes all token relationships into one single attention matrix ($A$), causing vector averaging on long contexts.
- **Multi-Head Model**: Splits 16 dimensions into 4 independent heads ($d_{\text{head}} = 4$). Computes 4 separate softmax distributions ($A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4$), preventing vector dilution and enabling parallel subspace searchlights!

